// After Hours' game schema (docs/WORLDKIT.md, "Game data and stable IDs"). Owned by agent B;
// agent A lists what the runtime needs in carts/afterhours/NOTES_A.md, and B's answers and decisions
// are in NOTES_B.md.
//
// A world holds one level: its rooms side by side, each in its own cell, at its own floor height
// (NOTES_B.md, "Level 1: how the rooms join"). An entity belongs to the room whose box (the room
// entity's position and size) contains its position in x and z.
//
// Conventions for every type:
// - Lengths in metres, one world unit a metre. Positions are world positions; a room's floor is
//   at its room entity's y, and every entity of the room stands at that height.
// - Times in ticks, 60 a second, counted on the room's clock (0 when the thief comes through the
//   room's door).
// - Directions in degrees, as a placement's yaw: 0 faces +z (away from the camera), 90 faces +x
//   (right on the screen), 180 faces -z (toward the camera), 270 (or -90) faces -x.
// - A box (`size`) is centred on the entity's position in x and z and rises size.y from it, and
//   is turned by the entity's yaw.

game afterhours

probe {
  radius            = 0.3
  height            = 1.7
  floor_max_degrees = 40
}

worlds level1, level2, platform

// A room. The entity's position is the centre of the room's floor (its y is the floor's height)
// and `size` its extent (x, y, z), which the camera's pan keeps on the screen and which holds the
// room's entities. `name` is what the transition, the stage select and the stage's end call it,
// as a World Kit name (lower case, words joined by _): shown in capitals with spaces, so
// `the_last_train` reads THE LAST TRAIN.
// `toys` and `bells` are the tools the thief has in the room.
//
// Rooms join in one of two ways:
// - In a line (level 1, the platform): `entry` is the spawn the thief starts and restarts at,
//   `exit` the exit box that clears the room; the thief then goes on to `next`, whose clock
//   starts at 0 (none: the stage is over). The stage starts at the room marked `first`.
// - By doors (stage 2): the `door` entities in a room say where each way out leads, and the
//   stage starts at the `first` room's `entry`. `next` then only orders the rooms (the section,
//   left to right; the stage select, the end, the save and the tapes number rooms by it), and
//   `exit` is none. `entry` in a room other than the first is unused (the scripts name one of
//   its doors' spawns).
type room {
  name:  name
  entry: ref
  exit:  ref?
  size:  vec3
  next:  ref?
  first: bool = false
  toys:  u8 = 0
  bells: u8 = 0
}

// Where the thief starts and restarts: the foot of the stair, the room's door. In a stage of
// doors, each door he can come in by has one (the door's `spawn`).
type spawn {
  yaw: fixed = 0
}

// The way out (the staff door). The room is cleared when the thief's centre is inside the box.
type exit {
  size: vec3 = [1, 2, 1]
}

// A point on a patrol guard's route. The guard walks to it, stops `wait` ticks looking `look`
// degrees, then walks on to `next` (none: the route ends here and the guard stays, looking `look`).
// While walking he looks where he walks.
type waypoint {
  next: ref?
  wait: s16 = 0
  look: fixed = 0
}

// A watcher. Its sight is a fan `sight` metres long and `fov` degrees wide, centred on where it
// looks, cut by walls (`wall` entities that block, and trains) and not by glass.
//
// - patrol: starts at its position and walks to `route` (its first waypoint), then round the
//   waypoints at `speed` metres a second.
// - posted: stands at its position and turns its head between `look_a` and `look_b`: it looks
//   `look_a` for `hold` ticks, turns to `look_b` in `turn` ticks, holds `hold` ticks, turns back
//   in `turn` ticks, and so on. `phase` ticks of that cycle have already passed at clock 0.
// - camera: as posted, and it cannot leave its post or hear. Its position is the lens, high on a
//   wall; the fan is drawn on the floor.
//
// How gentle a watcher is: `speed` (a patrol's walk), `sight` (the cone's length), `fov` (its
// width) and `turn_rate` (how fast a patrol's head turns toward where he walks or looks, degrees a
// second). Level 1's early rooms use slow patrols, short and narrow cones and slow heads.
//
// Patrol and posted guards hear noise: they walk to it, look around and walk back (DESIGN.md,
// "Caught means restart"). `hear` is how far, in metres, they hear the thief running; a tool's
// noise carries as far as the tool says (A's runtime).
type guard {
  kind:   patrol | posted | camera
  route:  ref?
  sight:  fixed = 6
  fov:    u8 = 60
  speed:  fixed = 1.2
  look_a: fixed = 0
  look_b: fixed = 0
  hold:   s16 = 60
  turn:   s16 = 90
  phase:  s16 = 0
  hear:   fixed = 6
  turn_rate: fixed = 240
}

// A train on its timetable. It runs along the World Kit path `path` (the track's centre line at
// the platform's height), from its first point (the tunnel mouth it comes out of) to its last (the
// one it leaves by); only the part of the train on the path exists (is drawn, blocks cones). Its
// head reaches the path's first point at tick `offset`, and again every `period` ticks. It slows to
// a stop `arrive` ticks later with its head `stop` metres along the path, opens its doors for
// `dwell` ticks, then speeds up and is gone from the path (its tail past the last point) `leave`
// ticks after the doors close. It is `cars` carriages of `car_length` metres, `width` metres wide,
// coupled without gaps; each carriage has `doors` doors on each side `sides` names (left and right
// of the way it travels), `door_width` metres wide, evenly spaced. The band along the path,
// `width` wide, is out of bounds to the thief and the guards except inside a stopped train with its
// doors open, which the thief enters and leaves by its doors; the doors close on a thief inside:
// caught. While on the path the train blocks every cone across it. The motion between the stops
// (easing in and out) is the runtime's (NOTES_A.md).
type train {
  path:       name
  period:     s16
  offset:     s16 = 0
  arrive:     s16 = 240
  stop:       fixed
  dwell:      s16 = 480
  leave:      s16 = 240
  cars:       u8 = 2
  car_length: fixed = 10
  width:      fixed = 2.6
  doors:      u8 = 3
  door_width: fixed = 1.3
  sides:      left | right | both = both
}

// Somewhere to hide: a box. The thief inside it who presses A is hidden from every cone until he
// moves out.
type hide_spot {
  size: vec3 = [1, 2, 1]
}

// Something fragile (a stack of cans, a sign on an easel). Brushing it makes a noise `noise`
// metres round, once, and changes nothing else. `radius` is its footprint.
type fragile {
  radius: fixed = 0.4
  noise:  fixed = 8
}

// The room's walls, as boxes in plan (2D): the wall map, written by the room's script from the
// same numbers as the room's assets, so the two cannot drift apart. `blocks` cuts cones; `solid`
// stops the thief and the guards. Glass is a wall that does not block (a tank, a window); low
// furniture (a bench) is the same: it stops the thief and the cones pass over it. The tracks are
// not walls: the runtime makes each train's path, `width` wide, out of bounds by itself.
type wall {
  size:   vec3
  blocks: bool = true
  solid:  bool = true
}

// A doorway (stage 2): a box `size` (x across the opening, z through it, turned by the yaw) in a
// gap of the room's wall map. The thief goes through it when his centre is inside the box, as
// through an exit: into the room of the door `to`, at that door's `spawn`, with that room's
// clock at 0; or, when `end`, out of the stage. He comes in by this door at `spawn` (none: it is
// not a way in), which stands clear of the box, and a caught thief starts again there.
//
// A staff door has a `card` colour. Shut, it is a wall across its box that blocks cones and
// stops the thief and the guards; the thief holding a card of its colour opens it by walking
// into it, and it stays open for the run (its `to` too). A door with no card is always open.
// The thief's cards and the doors he has opened become the run's when he leaves the room: caught,
// or START, he is back at the spawn he came in by and the room is as it was when he came in.
// A door with neither `to` nor `end` is a staff door inside a room: open, it is only a gap.
type door {
  size:  vec3 = [1.2, 2.2, 0.4]
  spawn: ref?
  to:    ref?
  end:   bool = false
  card:  none | red | blue | green | yellow = none
}

// A keycard on the floor (stage 2): picked up when the thief's centre comes within 0.5 m of it,
// shown on the HUD, and kept (not used up): it opens every staff door of its colour.
type keycard {
  colour: red | blue | green | yellow
}
